.vscode/
.claude/

build*/
cache/

logs/
screenshots/

# `assets/` holds two different things and they want opposite answers: the art
# the engine was developed against, which is gigabytes and belongs to nobody but
# this machine, and the handful of small files the engine and its documents
# actually ship. So the directory is excluded and the shipped subdirectories are
# named, rather than excluded wholesale and force-added one file at a time -
# which is what it was, and which meant `git add` silently refused a new logo.
#
# Git cannot re-include a path whose parent directory is excluded, so this has to
# be `assets/*` plus exceptions rather than `assets/` plus exceptions.
assets/*
!assets/logo/
!assets/fonts/
!assets/docs/

# The logo pipeline keeps its recipe rather than its intermediates, which is the
# trade the asset library and make_multimesh_rig.py already make: build_logos.py
# is tracked, and the shapes it reads and the previews it writes are not.
assets/logo/_preview/*
!assets/logo/_preview/build_logos.py
assets/logo/source_split/

# Per-user editor state. It belongs in ProjectPaths::userRoot(), but that root
# falls back to the engine root when the platform names no user directory or
# names one that is not writable - and a dev checkout is where that lands.
editor_recents.json
imgui.ini

editor_settings.json

# Derived cooked-asset cache (regenerated by the editor cooker)
/scenes/
/cooked/
/library/
# A project's derived caches, written by the cooker on save and on cook.
# A project's own source art. The blanket `assets/` pattern used to catch these
# by matching at any depth; now that it is anchored to the engine's own, they are
# named. physics_lab is the exception below - its world is authored rather than
# built at play time, so its art is source.
examples/*/assets/
examples/*/cooked/
examples/*/library/
# A project's built gameplay module, and the copies ScriptModule loads from.
examples/*/bin/
# Where vkm package assembles a standalone game, by default.
examples/*/dist/
# Our own working plan for where the engine is going. Not part of the manual.
docs/roadmap.md

# Throwaway verification harnesses; never part of the engine.
scratchpad/

# The physics lab is the one example whose world is authored rather than built
# at play time, so unlike the others it cannot make its own contents. Its source
# art and its asset recipes are both source: the scene stores names, and the
# recipes under library/ are what turn those names back into assets. Without
# either, a clone opens the project to an empty world. The cooked binaries
# beside them stay ignored - those are a cache the cooker rebuilds.
!examples/physics_lab/assets/
!examples/physics_lab/library/

# The `assets/` pattern above is unanchored, so it also hides the empty asset
# directory the project template ships - and `vkm new` then produces a project
# missing a directory its own README tells the user is there.
!templates/default/assets/
